413:- `root.go` sets `SilenceErrors: true` globally - Cobra never prints errors
763:- **Checkpoint egress trust gate** - `settings.CheckpointEgressAllowed(ctx)` is the single predicate for whether checkpoint data may leave the machine: explicit repo-level activation intent (a top-level `enabled` key) passes; a globally-enrolled repo passes only when trusted (`entire trust`, the three-option pre-push prompt — "Yes — trust this repo / Not now — re-ask next push / Always — trust every repo (trust_all)" — or `global.trust_all`); errors fail closed. Settings files created for unrelated features do not grant egress. Both egress entry points are gated — `ManualCommitStrategy.prePush` (early return beside the single-remote gate, above both backend branches, so held repos never run OPF) and `PushQueuedCheckpointRefs`. A hold never blocks the user's own push: the branch pushes, checkpoint data stays local (refs-backend refs stay queued) with one stderr explanation, and the first trusted push drains the backlog.
766:- **OPF's `command` is a trust boundary, not a setting**: `redaction.openai_privacy_filter.command` becomes `argv[0]` of an exec during pre-push, and `.entire/settings.json` is version-controlled — so reading it from the project file would let a pull request execute code on every developer who pushes (the prompt is no defense: it never names the command, `prompt_default: "always"` skips it, and non-TTY pushes auto-run). `settings.enforceOPFCommandTrust` (`settings/opf_command_trust.go`) honors `command` only from `.entire/settings.local.json`, and only when that file is untracked in **both** the index and `HEAD` — the filename is not the check, because `.gitignore` does not apply to an already-tracked path. The probe goes through go-git (`gitrepo.OpenPath`), not the git CLI, and is memoized per process: shelling out cost ~15 subprocesses per hook (`settings.Load` is uncached and runs several times per hook) and would fail verification wherever `git` is off `$PATH` — the GUI-git-client population that most needs an explicit `command`. **Two depths**: the layer check reads the index only, because a PR-delivered file is always in the index of a clone that checks it out, and checkout cannot produce a file absent from the index; the `command` check also reads HEAD. That split matters because HEAD is the expensive half — measured 8.3ms → 2.5ms per hook process in this repo, and 39ms → 11ms on reftable, where `gitrepo` routes reference reads back through the git CLI. The cost falls on everyone with a `settings.local.json`, not just OPF users, so keep it off the HEAD path. Rejection is a downgrade to the documented `$PATH` default plus a warning, never a hard error; verification failure (no repo, git missing) counts as untrusted. Do not add other exec-bearing fields to the project settings file without the same gate.
777:- `settings/opf_command_trust.go` - Ownership gate for the executed OPF `command` (local-only + untracked); see the OPF trust-boundary bullet above
782:Note: `checkpoint/configloader.go` overrides go-git's default config loader with a symlink-following `billy.Basic` (`osSymlinkFS`) — go-git's default reads config via `os.Root`, which rejects absolute symlinks in any path component (e.g. a `~/.config` managed by a dotfile tool), silently dropping global config so author identity fell back to "Unknown" and signing was skipped.
